<section class="section">
  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    A task is a prompt. Give it a trigger and the server runs it by itself,
    against any OpenAI-compatible model, with the tools of every MCP server you
    have connected. There is no conversation here, only tasks that fire on their
    own, and a record of what each one did. One process serves the web app, a
    GraphQL API, and the same API as MCP tools for other agents.
  </p>

  <ul class="card-grid">
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Cron and webhooks</h3>
        <p>A task fires on a schedule, or when a <code>POST</code> arrives at its webhook, and the JSON body of that request can be placed in the prompt.</p>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Your model, your tools</h3>
        <p>Point it at Ollama, LM Studio, OpenAI or OpenRouter, add stdio or HTTP MCP servers, and every run can reach their tools.</p>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Every run kept</h3>
        <p>Status, output, tool calls and token counts are stored for each run, and a run in flight can be watched as it happens.</p>
      </article>
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="section" id="start">
  <h2>Get started</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <pre class="code"><code>git clone https://github.com/cubicecho/task_server.git
cd task_server
docker compose up --build</code></pre>
    <p>
      Open <a href="http://localhost:8787">http://localhost:8787</a>, go to
      <strong>Settings</strong>, point the base URL at an OpenAI-compatible
      server and pick a model. Then create a task, give it a cron expression,
      and press <strong>Run now</strong>. The database is embedded, so there is
      nothing else to install.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="readme-link">
    Full documentation lives in the project README:
    <a href="{{ site.repo }}#readme" rel="noopener">{{ site.repo }}</a>
  </p>
</section>
